---
name: no-silent-fallback
description: Never swallow errors or skip work silently; unexpected state must not be hidden.
---

# No Silent Fallback

Code must never hide a failure or an unexpected state behind a silent success.

## Exception handling

Do not:

- rescue and ignore errors
- use `rescue nil`
- catch a broad exception only to continue as if nothing happened

Reason: swallowed exceptions hide failures and turn a hard error into silent data corruption.

## No "skip if missing" guards

Do not wrap an operation in a guard that silently no-ops when state is unexpected, for example a
`rename_table` wrapped in `return unless table_exists?(...)`. A silent skip hides a partial or failed
operation and lets state drift until it surfaces much later as a confusing failure.

When state may be inconsistent, fail loudly instead (see `fail-fast`).

## No masking default-value fallbacks

Do not substitute a default value to paper over a missing or invalid input when its absence means
something is actually wrong. A fallback is only acceptable when the absent value is a legitimate,
expected case — never as a way to avoid handling an error.
